  "text": "Here's a complete implementation of the `remove_adjacent_duplicates` function, along with the provided `unittest` tests. This function iterates through the list and builds a new list by including an element only if it's different from the previous one \u2014 that is, it removes consecutive duplicates.\n\n```python\nimport unittest\n\ndef remove_adjacent_duplicates(lst):\n    if not lst:  # Handle empty list\n        return []\n    \n    result = [lst[0]]  # Start with the first element\n    for num in lst[1:]:\n        if num != result[-1]:\n            result.append(num)\n    return result\n\nclass TestRemoveAdjacentDuplicates(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_basic_functionality(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]), [1, 2, 3, 4])\n    \n    def test_repeated_elements(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([5, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7]), [5, 6, 7])\n    \n    def test_multiple_duplicates(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5]), [1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n\n- The function starts by checking if the list is empty and returns an empty list if it is.\n- It then initializes a new list `result` with the first element of the input list.\n- It then iterates through the rest of the list, appending an element to `result` only if it is different from the last element in `result`.\n- This effectively removes any consecutive duplicates, leaving only one instance of each group of consecutive duplicates.\n\n### Example:\nFor the input `[1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]`, the output is `[1, 2, 3, 4]`.\n\nYou can run this script directly, and it will execute the test cases using `unittest`.",
